#
# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
#   ./build.sh linux tsan
#   FNL_BACKEND=wayland TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=$PWD/tsan.supp \
#       ./build/linux/tsan/fenestal-testbed
#
# The same file serves the same tree on X11. There the render thread and the
# event pump hold the *same* connection and libxcb takes its own locks, so a
# report naming libxcb is something to read, not something to add an entry for.
#
# TSAN_OPTIONS is one variable: anything else wanted in it is appended to the
# line above, or the suppressions are dropped and the RADV report comes back.
#
# With this file the program runs clean -- no races, no lock-order warnings,
# exit status 0. An entry added here says what report it was checked against
# and why it cannot cover this project's own code.

# ---- RADV's disk-cache thread pool -----------------------------------------
# The driver races itself: pthread_barrier_destroy on one of the threads
# vkCreateDevice spawns, against pthread_barrier_wait on another. Both frames
# are inside libvulkan_radeon.so; nothing of ours is on either stack.
#
# Keyed on the barrier calls and not on the library. The innermost frame is
# TSan's own interceptor, so `race_top:libvulkan_radeon.so` never matches, and
# a plain `race:libvulkan_radeon.so` would match every Vulkan call this program
# makes. This project uses no pthread barrier of its own -- `grep -r
# pthread_barrier src/` -- so these cannot hide anything of ours. radeonsi
# runs the same pool under OpenGL: 13 matches in a wayland-gl testbed's
# few seconds.
race_top:pthread_barrier_destroy
race_top:pthread_barrier_wait

# ---- a trap worth naming ---------------------------------------------------
# Do NOT add `called_from_lib:libvulkan_radeon.so` as a tidier form of the
# above. It makes TSan ignore accesses made *from* that library, and RADV's
# Wayland WSI calls into libwayland to commit each present -- so the
# happens-before that libwayland's display mutex establishes is thrown away,
# and libwayland-client is then reported racing with itself, both accesses
# holding the same mutex.

# ---- NVK's GPU address heap ------------------------------------------------
# Checked against the x11-vulkan-bare round on schenker's GTX 1050 Ti (NVK,
# Mesa 26.2.4, Arch): a free in util_vma_heap_free() on the render thread,
# retiring a swapchain's images, against the calloc of the same block in
# util_vma_heap_free() on the reaper's vkDestroySwapchainKHR. Both are the
# driver returning GPU virtual addresses to its heap, under the device's own
# lock -- a simple_mtx, a bare futex TSan does not see.
#
# Keyed on that function, which is Mesa's (src/util/vma.c) and not ours, and
# manages the driver's own list of free ranges: no memory of this project is
# read or written inside it. It matches only where the driver's debug info is
# installed -- the distributions strip the function's name, and without it
# the frame is <null> and the report fails the round as before.
# tools/drive/bare_driver.py --setup fetches it (DEBUGINFO_WHY).
race:util_vma_heap_free

# ---- radeonsi under OpenGL: the driver's own hand-offs ---------------------
# Checked against the wayland-gl tsan round on this desk's RX 590 (radeonsi,
# Mesa 26.0.8-1ubuntu0.3), the driver's frames named from Ubuntu's
# mesa-libgallium-dbgsym. Neither stack has a frame of this project's own
# above the GL entry point it called, and neither block is ours.
#
# A shader binary read from the driver's disk cache by read_chunk()
# (radeonsi/si_state_shaders.cpp) on its compiler thread, `<program>:sh0`,
# and copied into GPU memory by si_shader_binary_upload_at() on whichever
# thread links the program -- here the main thread, in gl_create_overlay()
# -- under the shader's own mutex. The compiler thread hands the shader over
# through a util_queue fence, a bare futex TSan does not see.
race:si_shader_binary_upload_at
#
# One of Mesa's hash tables, made with the context and cleared by
# _mesa_hash_table_clear() both on the driver's own thread,
# `<program>:gdrv0`, and on the thread that destroys the context: two
# writes, ordered by the threaded context's batch fences, the same futex.
#
# It covers more than that report, and that is why it is safe: the name
# matches anywhere on either stack, on every platform, and with it whatever
# the clear's delete callback does. Every table it clears is Mesa's and
# every callback it runs is Mesa's -- this project hands Mesa neither, and
# reaches the function only through a GL or Vulkan entry point -- so what
# it hides is the driver's own state, never this project's. Keyed on the
# function and not on race_top, which names TSan's memset interceptor.
race:_mesa_hash_table_clear
#
# The command stream's own buffers, the same context's hand-off again: the
# render thread's gl_draw_frame() and the driver's <program>:gdrv0 thread
# both flush and grow the CS (amdgpu_cs_flush(), amdgpu_do_add_buffer())
# and begin a new one (si_begin_new_gfx_cs()), ordered by the batch fences
# the threaded context hands a frame over through. Seen on the x11-vulkan
# tsan round (2026-11-06), whose GPU box moves the window onto radeonsi's
# OpenGL: every frame between TSan's interceptor and gl_draw_frame() is
# Mesa's, no block is this project's, and the library reaches the three
# only through the GL entry point it calls.
race:amdgpu_cs_flush
race:amdgpu_do_add_buffer
race:si_begin_new_gfx_cs
#
# All match only where the driver's debug info is installed, as the NVK
# entry above does: without it the frames are <null> and the report fails
# the round as before.

# ---- Zink and lavapipe on schenker: the drivers' own hand-overs ----------
# Checked against the two KMS tsan rounds on schenker's GTX 1050 Ti on
# nouveau (Mesa 26.2.5, LLVM 23.1.3, Arch, 2026-10-26), the frames named
# from the debug info by build id: OpenGL drawn by Zink over NVK, by iris
# and by llvmpipe, and Vulkan by lavapipe. On every stack below, each
# frame between TSan's and the GL or Vulkan entry point of ours is Mesa's
# or LLVM's, and every block is the driver's own.
#
# Zink's shader cache. Its cache thread, `<program>:zcfq0`, serialises a
# program's NIR into a blob (grow_to_fit(), blob_write_bytes()), and the
# render thread reads it back inside the frame's readback, read_window()
# (blob_read_string(), read_variable() in nir_serialize.c) -- and,
# rebuilding at a move, destroys the program (deinit_program()) while
# cache_put_job() reads its state. The thread hands both over through a
# util_queue fence, a bare futex TSan does not see, as radeonsi's
# compiler thread does above.
race:grow_to_fit
race:blob_write_bytes
race:blob_read_string
race:read_variable
race:cache_put_job
#
# The GLSL type cache: a record type's name written by linear_strdup() on
# one of Zink's threads and compared by record_key_compare() and
# glsl_record_compare() on another, both under the cache's simple_mtx.
race:record_key_compare
race:glsl_record_compare
#
# lavapipe's shader compiles, on two of the threads its vkCreateDevice
# starts, inside LLVM's uniquing tables (FunctionType::get(),
# StringMapImpl::LookupBucketFor(), IEEEFloat::bitwiseIsEqual()). Both
# stacks are the driver's threads alone, with no frame of ours. Keyed on
# LLVM's library: nothing of this project calls LLVM, so a stack through
# it is a compile of Mesa's and never a race on memory of ours.
race:libLLVM.so
#
# lavapipe's _mesa_hash_table_copy() between two of its rasteriser
# threads, `llvmpipe-3` and `-4`, a read against a write of the same
# ralloc block one of them made: both stacks are lavapipe's alone, the
# threads made with the device and nothing of ours above the copy
# (kms-vulkan-bare@schenker:nouveau under tsan, Mesa 26.2.6, 2026-10-31).
# Its memcpy is inlined into the function, which the symbolizer named in
# one run of two with Mesa 26.2.6's debug info installed and left <null>
# in the other, the same boot and files: named where lavapipe was loaded
# before TSan's first frame was, which the preload below now makes so
# every time (bare_driver's TSAN_PRELOADED). Keyed on the function, as
# _mesa_hash_table_clear above is and for its reason: every table it
# copies is Mesa's, reached only through a Vulkan or GL entry point.
race:_mesa_hash_table_copy
#
# What Zink over NVK meets once NVK's frames are named (the bare
# machines' rounds preload the drivers under TSan, bare_driver's
# TSAN_PRELOADED: GCC's symbolizer names no library loaded after its first
# frame). Checked against kms-gl-bare@schenker:nouveau under tsan, the GTX
# 1050 Ti on nouveau, Mesa 26.2.6 with its debug info, 2026-10-31; every
# frame between TSan's and the GL entry point of ours (glBlitFramebuffer in
# gl_bridge_scan_show(), eglDestroyContext in destroy_device_objects()) is
# Zink's, NVK's or Mesa's runtime's, and none of these names is ours
# (`grep -rn` over src/ and examples/).
#
# Zink compiling a pipeline on the render thread, inside the GL call,
# against its own compile thread `<program>:zcfq*` building the same
# program's library (zink_create_pipeline_lib(), create_program()): Zink's
# state, handed over through a util_queue fence. Seen once in two rounds.
race:zink_create_gfx_pipeline_combined
# NVK's pipeline cache: an entry made by vk_raw_data_cache_object_create()
# on Zink's cache thread, against the cache destroyed with the context. A
# VkPipelineCache is internally synchronized, by the specification, so a
# race inside one is the driver's whatever the caller does. Five matches
# in a kms-vulkan round (print_suppressions=1).
race:vk_raw_data_cache_object_create
#
# With the drivers preloaded the keys above it match too: in that round
# blob_write_bytes 270 times, blob_read_string 114, libLLVM.so 88,
# read_variable 24, cache_put_job 6, _mesa_hash_table_copy 4. Four rounds
# then passed clean, kms-vulkan-bare and kms-gl-bare twice each, 71 checks.
#
# Not keyed: the gallium threaded context's driver thread, `<program>:gdrv0`,
# against the render thread on one of NVK's command buffers
# (nvk_BeginCommandBuffer(), vk_dynamic_graphics_state_clear(), the push
# buffer's map; and Zink's rendering info and the blitter's saved states,
# both sides on it). The threaded context hands a batch over through a futex
# TSan does not see; but a key on NVK's command-buffer functions would also
# hide a command buffer of our own Vulkan renderer's recorded on two
# threads. A tsan round runs Mesa's OpenGL with GALLIUM_THREAD=0 instead
# (bare_driver), which makes no such thread: the GL calls run on the thread
# that made them, ours.

# ---- NVIDIA's EGL against its own Wayland platform library -----------------
# A lock-order inversion between two of NVIDIA's libraries, and between two
# moments that never overlap. Every EGL call on a Wayland display takes
# libnvidia-egl-wayland's static lock and then one of libEGL_nvidia's -- the
# GPU lister's eglInitialize in list_devices() (src/renderer/gl/egl.c) is the
# first to. At process exit, libEGL_nvidia's own teardown takes its lock and
# then calls into libnvidia-egl-wayland, which takes the static one: the
# cycle closes there, with no frame of ours on the main thread's stack --
# main() has returned. The lister is joined in render_thread_stop(), so
# nothing of ours is inside EGL by then. Checked against the testbed on
# weston with NVIDIA's 580.196.07 (FNL_GPU=intel, the lister listing the
# 1050 Ti's OpenGL): the report closes 0.35 s after SIGTERM, ten seconds
# after the listing. Keyed on the Wayland library, which is in both of the
# static lock's stacks and in nothing this project's locks are taken from.
deadlock:libnvidia-egl-wayland.so
